(   [Problem Statement]: Explain JavaScript variable scoping paradigms, comparing var, let, and const in terms of scope, hoisting, and re-declaration.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   JavaScript provides three variable declaration keywords with fundamentally different scoping) Tj
T*
(   and lifecycle rules:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. var \(ES1 - Legacy\):) Tj
T*
(   - Scope: Function-scoped \(or globally scoped if declared outside a function\). Ignores block) Tj
T*
(   boundaries \(if blocks, for loops\).) Tj
T*
(   - Hoisting: Hoisted to the top of its containing function scope and initialized with the value) Tj
T*
(   `undefined`. Can be accessed before its declaration line without throwing an error.) Tj
T*
(   - Re-declaration: Allows re-declaring the same variable multiple times in the same scope) Tj
T*
(   without error, causing bugs.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. let \(ES6 - Modern Standard\):) Tj
T*
(   - Scope: Strictly Block-scoped \(enclosed within curly braces { }\).) Tj
T*
(   - Hoisting: Hoisted to the top of the block, but NOT initialized. Accessing it before) Tj
T*
(   declaration triggers a ReferenceError due to the Temporal Dead Zone \(TDZ\).) Tj
T*
(   - Re-declaration: Prohibits re-declaring the same variable within the same block scope.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. const \(ES6 - Modern Standard\):) Tj
T*
(   - Scope: Strictly Block-scoped.) Tj
T*
(   - Initialization: Must be initialized immediately upon declaration.) Tj
T*
(   - Immutability: The variable identifier cannot be reassigned. However, if the value is an) Tj
T*
(   Object or Array, the internal contents remain mutable \(reference immutability, not value) Tj
T*
(   immutability\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
