(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze the Temporal Dead Zone \(TDZ\) in JavaScript, explaining why it exists and illustrating its behavior with code.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   The Temporal Dead Zone \(TDZ\) is the period between entering a block scope and the actual) Tj
T*
(   execution of the let or const declaration statement:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Why TDZ Exists:) Tj
T*
(   TC39 introduced the TDZ in ECMAScript 2015 to catch runtime programming bugs where variables) Tj
T*
(   are read before they are defined, and to ensure const variables are never observed in an) Tj
T*
(   uninitialized state.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Illustrative Code Comparison:) Tj
T*
(   ```javascript) Tj
T*
(   // Behavior with var:) Tj
T*
(   console.log\(a\); // Outputs: undefined \(hoisted & initialized\)) Tj
T*
(   var a = 10;) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   // Behavior with let / const:) Tj
T*
(   // TDZ begins for variable 'b') Tj
T*
(   console.log\(b\); // Throws: ReferenceError: Cannot access 'b' before initialization) Tj
T*
(   let b = 20; // TDZ ends for variable 'b') Tj
T*
(   console.log\(b\); // Outputs: 20) Tj
T*
ET

