(   [Problem Statement]: Explain JavaScript Data Types, detailing Primitive Types vs Reference Types and the memory model \(Stack vs Heap\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   JavaScript categorizes values into two primary memory models:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. Primitive Types \(Value Types\):) Tj
T*
(   - Includes: Number, BigInt, String, Boolean, Undefined, Null, and Symbol.) Tj
T*
(   - Storage: Stored directly in Stack memory as fixed-size, lightweight primitive values.) Tj
T*
(   - Copying: Copied by Value. Assigning `let y = x;` duplicates the actual value. Modifying `y`) Tj
T*
(   has zero effect on `x`.) Tj
T*
(   - Immutability: Primitive values are inherently immutable. Operations return a new primitive) Tj
T*
(   value rather than altering the original.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Reference Types \(Objects\):) Tj
T*
(   - Includes: Plain Objects \({ }\), Arrays \([ ]\), Functions, Dates, and RegExps.) Tj
T*
(   - Storage: The actual object data structure resides in dynamic Heap memory. The variable on the) Tj
T*
(   Stack stores only a pointer \(reference memory address\) to the heap location.) Tj
T*
(   - Copying: Copied by Reference. Assigning `let obj2 = obj1;` copies only the memory pointer.) Tj
T*
(   Mutating `obj2.name` mutates the underlying heap object, directly affecting `obj1`.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
