(   [Problem Statement]: Explain JavaScript Object Immutability techniques: Object.preventExtensions\(\), Object.seal\(\), and Object.freeze\(\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   JavaScript provides three graduated levels of object immutability via static Object methods:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. Object.preventExtensions\(obj\):) Tj
T*
(   - Prevents new properties from being added to `obj`.) Tj
T*
(   - Existing properties CAN still be modified \(values changed\) and deleted.) Tj
T*
(   - Check: `Object.isExtensible\(obj\)`.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Object.seal\(obj\):) Tj
T*
(   - Prevents new properties from being added AND prevents existing properties from being deleted.) Tj
T*
(   - Marks all existing properties as `configurable: false`.) Tj
T*
(   - Existing writable property values CAN still be mutated/changed.) Tj
T*
(   - Check: `Object.isSealed\(obj\)`.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Object.freeze\(obj\):) Tj
T*
(   - Highest level of native immutability.) Tj
T*
(   - Seals the object AND marks all existing properties as `writable: false`.) Tj
T*
(   - No additions, no deletions, and NO value modifications.) Tj
T*
(   - Check: `Object.isFrozen\(obj\)`.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   Shallow Immutability Caveat: All three methods are strictly shallow. Nested objects inside a) Tj
T*
(   frozen parent remain fully mutable unless deep-frozen recursively.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
