(   [Problem Statement]: Explain JavaScript Symbols, detailing Symbol.for\(\), Symbol.keyFor\(\), and Symbol as non-enumerable object metadata.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Symbols are guaranteed-unique primitive values introduced in ES6:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. Local Symbols:) Tj
T*
(   `const sym1 = Symbol\('id'\); const sym2 = Symbol\('id'\); sym1 === sym2; // false`) Tj
T*
(   Ideal for collision-free object property keys that external code cannot overwrite by accident.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Global Symbol Registry:) Tj
T*
(   - `Symbol.for\('token'\)`: Searches global registry; creates symbol if missing, or returns) Tj
T*
(   existing symbol if present.) Tj
T*
(   - `Symbol.keyFor\(sym\)`: Returns the string description key for a global registry symbol.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Non-Enumerable Visibility:) Tj
T*
(   Symbol keys are skipped by `for...in`, `Object.keys\(\)`, and `JSON.stringify\(\)`. They are) Tj
T*
(   accessed explicitly via `Object.getOwnPropertySymbols\(obj\)` or `Reflect.ownKeys\(obj\)`.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
